/*!
    \example simple_service
    \title Simple Service
    \ingroup qtros2-examples
    \brief Answering std_srvs/SetBool requests from QML with a single
    service server.

    \c{Simple Service} shows the server side of a ROS 2 service in QML: it wraps
    one \l{Qt ROS2 std_srvs QML Types}{SetBoolServiceServer} in a \l{Qt ROS2 Core
    QML Types}{Node} and switches a "lamp" on or off whenever a
    \c std_srvs/SetBool request arrives — from the \l{Simple Service Client}
    example, from \c{ros2 service call}, or from any other ROS 2 node.

    \image simple_service/simple_service.png "The Simple Service window: an editable topic, the lamp that requests switch on and off, a deferred-reply switch, and the last request."

    \section1 Running the Example

    \list 1
        \li Source your ROS 2 environment, then launch Qt Creator from the same
            shell so it can find the generated QML modules.
        \li Open \c examples/simple_service/CMakeLists.txt in Qt Creator, then
            build and run.
        \li Call the service from a separate, ROS-sourced terminal:
            \badcode
            ros2 service call /simple_service_lamp std_srvs/srv/SetBool "{data: true}"
            \endcode
    \endlist

    Pair this example with \l{Simple Service Client} to see both ends of a
    service in two Qt windows.

    \section1 Declaring a Service Server

    Like publishers and subscribers, a service server is declared as a child of
    a \c Node; the node owns the underlying \c rclcpp service and tears it down
    automatically. The \c handler function is called for every incoming
    request:

    \qml
    import QtRos2.StdSrvs

    Node {
        id: rosNode
        nodeName: "simple_service_node"

        SetBoolServiceServer {
            id: lampService
            topic: topicField.text

            handler: (request, reply) => {
                root.lampOn = request
                return { success: true, message: `lamp is now ${request ? "on" : "off"}` }
            }
        }
    }
    \endqml

    For \c std_srvs/SetBool the request payload is a single \c bool, so
    \c request arrives as a plain boolean. Services with multi-field requests
    receive the generated value type instead, with the same \c camelCase field
    access as messages. The response works symmetrically: a plain JavaScript
    object with the response fields (here \c success and \c message) is
    converted to the \c SetBool response automatically.

    The handler always runs on the GUI thread, so it can freely touch QML
    state — the ROS executor thread is never blocked, even while a response is
    still pending.

    \section1 Deferred Replies

    Returning the response directly is the right style for instant answers.
    When honoring a request takes time — moving an arm, speaking an utterance,
    waiting for hardware to settle — return \c undefined instead and keep the
    \c reply parameter; the caller receives the response only when
    \c{reply.send()} is called:

    \qml
    handler: (request, reply) => {
        root.lampOn = request
        replyTimer.pendingReplies.push(reply)
        replyTimer.restart()
        // no return statement: the response is sent later
    }
    \endqml

    \qml
    Timer {
        id: replyTimer
        interval: 1000
        property var pendingReplies: []
        onTriggered: {
            for (const reply of pendingReplies)
                reply.send({ success: true, message: "done (delayed reply)" })
            pendingReplies = []
        }
    }
    \endqml

    The example's \e{Delay the response} switch selects between the two styles
    at runtime, so you can observe from the calling terminal that the answer
    arrives one second late — while the server window stays fully responsive
    and continues to accept further requests.

    If no handler is set, or the handler throws, the server sends a
    default-constructed response so that callers never wait forever.

    \sa {Simple Service Client}, {Qt ROS2 std_srvs QML Types}
*/
